    They are mutually exclusive and the swap is a single class on this wrapper,
    set by applyFilters() below. Doing it this way is deliberate: the filtering
    code already owns #feed-grid entirely — it caches the grid's original HTML,
    rebuilds it from the <template>, and pages it with Load More. Rebuilding
    that machinery to page six independent horizontal rows would have been a
    rewrite of the one part of this component that currently works.

    So the rows are the browse view and the grid is the results view, which is
    also the right split for a reader: you scan rows when you have not decided
    what you want, and you want a plain list the moment you have.
  */}

  {/*
    ─── ONE ROW COMPONENT, RENDERED AT TWO DEPTHS ────────────────────────────

    The tentpole row sits on its own above the sections; every other row sits
    inside one. Both use the same `.feed-row` markup so a change to the rail,
    the snapping or the chevrons lands in both places at once.

    HEADING LEVELS: FeedLayout already emits the page h1 and an sr-only h2
    naming the surface, so section titles are h2 (siblings of it) and row
    titles are h3. Nothing skips a level, which is what axe reports on.
  */}

  {hasRows && (
    <>
      <FloatingPageNav entries={navItems} />
      <div id="feed-rows" data-hero-banner={collections[0]?.bannerArt ? "" : undefined}>

      {/*
        ─── CURATED COLLECTIONS ───────────────────────────────────────────────

        The Lanterns shelf, generalised. Identical treatment: full-bleed key
        art, the brand lockup, the accent wash across the whole row and tiles at
        the `featured` size. One per flagged series, so a second show can have
        one without touching this file.
      */}
      {collections.map((collection) => (
        <section
          class="feed-row feed-row--prestige feed-row--accented"
          id={collection.id}
          data-row={collection.id}
          data-collection
          style={`--row-accent-color: ${collection.accentColor}; --row-accent-rgb: ${collection.accentRgb};`}
        >
          {collection.bannerArt && (
            <FeedRowBanner
              art={collection.bannerArt}
              logo={collection.bannerLogo}
              title={collection.name}
              eyebrow={collection.eyebrow}
              accentRgb={collection.accentRgb}
              href={collection.hub?.url}
            />
          )}

          {/*
            ─── THE LOCKUP BELONGS TO WHICHEVER ELEMENT IS THERE, NOT BOTH ────

            FeedRowBanner already renders the eyebrow in words at its lower
            left, the series logo as art at its lower right, and the row's
            `<h2>` as sr-only beside the eyebrow. Rendering the lockup HERE as
            well put "FEATURED SERIES - DC" on screen twice and "LANTERNS"
            twice — once as the banner's logo and once as type — which is what
            was reported.

            FeedRowBanner.astro carried both decisions at once: its header note
            said the duplication was accepted for consistency, and a note
            further down said "the row head below now carries only its
            actions". The code followed the first and the comment promised the
            second. This is the second, which is also the owner's call on
            seeing it.

            A collection with NO banner art keeps the full lockup — otherwise
            that row would have no visible heading and no <h2> at all, since
            both live in the banner.
          */}
          <div class="feed-row-head feed-row-head--prestige">
            {!collection.bannerArt && (
              <div class="prestige-brand-lockup">
                <span class="prestige-eyebrow">{collection.eyebrow}</span>
                <div class="prestige-title-group">
                  {collection.hub?.logo ? (
                    <img src={collection.hub.logo} alt={collection.hub.title} class="prestige-mark" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
                  ) : (
                    collection.hub && <span class="prestige-badge">{collection.hub.title}</span>
                  )}
                  {collection.hub && <span class="prestige-divider" aria-hidden="true"></span>}
                  <h2 class="feed-row-title prestige-title">{collection.name}</h2>
                </div>
              </div>
            )}

            <div class="prestige-actions">
              {collection.hub && (
                <a href={collection.hub.url} class="prestige-hub-link">
                  <span>EXPLORE {collection.hub.title.toUpperCase()} HUB</span>
                  <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                    <path d="M5 12h14M12 5l7 7-7 7" />
                  </svg>
                </a>
              )}
              <div class="feed-row-nav" aria-hidden="true">
                <button type="button" class="feed-row-chevron" data-dir="-1" tabindex="-1">
                  <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                    <path d="M15 5l-7 7 7 7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" />
                  </svg>
                </button>
                <button type="button" class="feed-row-chevron" data-dir="1" tabindex="-1">
                  <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                    <path d="M9 5l7 7-7 7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" />
                  </svg>
                </button>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <ul class="feed-row-rail" data-rail>
            {collection.items.map((item: any, index: number) => (
              <li class="feed-row-item">
                <ContentCard
                  item={item}
                  index={index}
                  context="feed"
                  variant="featured"
                  brandOverride={collectionBrands.get(collection.id) ?? null}
                />
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </section>
      ))}

      {collections.length > 0 && (
        <div class="feed-rotator-break">
          <CommercialRotator />
        </div>
      )}

      {/*
        ─── THE RAILS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

        Flat, and visible. They were six collapsible SECTIONS containing
        fourteen rows, and the accordion existed because fourteen stacked rows
        made the page an endless scroll. Eight rails do not stack that way: a
        rail of seventeen stories is one row of vertical space, which is the
        whole reason a browse surface uses them. Nothing to collapse, so nothing
        to open.

        Same `.feed-row` markup as the collections above, so the rail, the
        snapping and the chevrons are one implementation.
      */}
      {rails.map((rail) => (
        <section class="feed-row feed-rail" id={rail.anchor ?? rail.id} data-row={rail.id}>
          <div class="feed-row-head">
            <h2 class="feed-row-title feed-rail-title">{rail.title}</h2>
            <div class="feed-row-actions">
              {rail.href && (
                <a href={rail.href} class="feed-hub-link">
                  <span>EXPLORE {rail.title.toUpperCase()}</span>
                  <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                    <path d="M5 12h14M12 5l7 7-7 7" />
                  </svg>
                </a>
              )}
              {/* Mouse affordances only. A touch device scrolls the rail
                  directly and a keyboard reaches every card through it. */}
              <div class="feed-row-nav" aria-hidden="true">
                <button type="button" class="feed-row-chevron" data-dir="-1" tabindex="-1">
                  <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                    <path d="M15 5l-7 7 7 7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" />
                  </svg>
                </button>
                <button type="button" class="feed-row-chevron" data-dir="1" tabindex="-1">
                  <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                    <path d="M9 5l7 7-7 7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" />
                  </svg>
                </button>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <ul class="feed-row-rail" data-rail>
            {rail.items.map((item: any, index: number) => (
              <li class="feed-row-item">
                <ContentCard item={item} index={index} context="feed" />
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </section>
      ))}
      </div>
    </>
  )}

  {!hasRows && (
    <EmptyState message={MESSAGES.noContent} actionLabel={LABELS.viewAllContent} actionHref="/feed" />
  )}
</div>

{/*
  The <template id="full-dataset-cards"> that stood here is gone. It rendered
  EVERY feed item a second time as full cards so the legacy hash filter could
  clone from it, which on /feed meant shipping 40 extra card subtrees the page
  never displayed. Removed for parse time and TBT.

  Nothing reads it any more: the filter row is no longer rendered on any /feed
  route (FeedLayout's `showFilters`), so there is no control that can set a
  filter, and the horizontal rows segment the same content by type and brand.
*/}



{/*
  ─── NO PAGINATION AND NO LOAD MORE ────────────────────────────────────────

  <Pagination /> used to render here, then a Load More button that replaced it.
  Both are gone from THIS component; the Pagination component itself still
  serves /intel, /category/*, /events, the events archive and event coverage.

  The rows carry every item already, so a "page 2" of a feed you scroll
  sideways is a second, contradictory way through the same content. Load More
  outlived it only to page the filtered grid, and that grid no longer exists.
*/}

<script>
  /*
    ─── THE CLIENT-SIDE HASH FILTER IS GONE ──────────────────────────────────

    ~300 lines of initFeedClientFiltering() stood here: it rebuilt #feed-grid
    from a <template> of the whole feed, paged it with Load More, kept the
    filter buttons' active states in step and normalised ?category= into a hash.

    Every input it had has been removed. The /feed routes no longer render a
    filter row, the template it cloned from is gone, and the grid it wrote into
    is gone — it had already been dead at runtime, bailing at its own
    `if (!feedGrid || !template) return;` on every page load.

    A stale /feed#film link now simply shows the full feed instead of a filtered
    grid with no way back to the rows, which is the better of the two.
  */

  /*
    THE ROW CHEVRONS.

    Bound per rail, and re-bound on every `astro:page-load` because ClientRouter
    swaps the document without reloading this module. Nothing is stored on
    `window` here (unlike the delegated handlers below) because these listeners
    live on elements that are themselves replaced by the swap, so they go with
    the nodes they were attached to.

    `scrollBy` gets 90% of the rail's visible width rather than a fixed pixel
    count, so one press advances by roughly one screenful of cards at any
    breakpoint, and the snap points do the final alignment.
  */
  function initFeedRows() {
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.feed-row').forEach((row) => {
      const rail = row.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-rail]');
      if (!rail) return;

      const chevrons = Array.from(row.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('.feed-row-chevron'));

      /* A rail that fits its content has nothing to scroll, so both chevrons
         are disabled rather than left live and inert-feeling. 1px of slack
         absorbs sub-pixel layout rounding, which otherwise leaves the right
         chevron permanently enabled on an exactly-fitting row. */
      /*
        ─── READS AND WRITES ARE SEPARATED, AND COALESCED ─────────────────────

        This runs on every scroll tick. It used to read layout (`scrollWidth`,
        `clientWidth`, `scrollLeft`) and then immediately write to the DOM
        (`setAttribute`, `setProperty`) in the same frame, which forces a
        synchronous re-layout on each tick — layout thrashing, straight into
        Total Blocking Time on a page carrying nine of these rails.

        Now every read happens first, and the writes are deferred to the next
        animation frame.

        `pending` is the second half of the fix and matters as much as the rAF:
        a scroll gesture fires this dozens of times, and scheduling a frame per
        tick just moves the pile-up rather than removing it. One frame is
        queued at a time and it writes the freshest values it was given.
      */
      let pending = false;
      let latest = { max: 0, left: 0 };

      const sync = () => {
        // 1. Read layout properties first.
        const max = rail.scrollWidth - rail.clientWidth;
        const left = rail.scrollLeft;
        latest = { max, left };

        if (pending) return;
        pending = true;

        // 2. Write to the DOM asynchronously.
        requestAnimationFrame(() => {
          pending = false;
          const scrollable = latest.max > 1;
          const atStart = latest.left <= 1;
          const atEnd = latest.left >= latest.max - 1;

          chevrons.forEach((btn) => {
            const dir = Number(btn.dataset.dir);
            btn.setAttribute('data-disabled', String(!scrollable || (dir < 0 ? atStart : atEnd)));
          });

          /*
            The feather, one side at a time. A side is faded only when there is
            actually content past it, so a rail resting at 0 has a crisp first
            card and a fade on the right. Derived from the SAME values as the
            chevrons above: the button being disabled and that edge being
            un-feathered are one fact, and computing them separately is how the
            two would drift.
          */
          rail.style.setProperty('--fade-start', !scrollable || atStart ? '0px' : 'var(--feed-fade)');
          rail.style.setProperty('--fade-end', !scrollable || atEnd ? '0px' : 'var(--feed-fade)');
        });
      };

      /*
        Mirror the rail's resolved inline padding onto its scroll padding. See
        the note on `scroll-padding-inline` in the stylesheet: the snapport
        otherwise starts at the padding-box edge and the snap engine scrolls
        the gutter away on load, landing the first card flush against the
        screen instead of on its heading's line.

        Read from the computed style rather than restated as a number, so the
        breakpoint that owns the gutter stays the only place it is defined.
      */
      const syncScrollPadding = () => {
        const { paddingLeft } = getComputedStyle(rail);
        rail.style.scrollPaddingInline = paddingLeft;
      };

      chevrons.forEach((btn) => {
        btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
          const dir = Number(btn.dataset.dir) || 1;
          rail.scrollBy({ left: dir * rail.clientWidth * 0.9, behavior: 'smooth' });
        });
      });

      /*
        ─── WHICH TILE IS THE READER LOOKING AT ──────────────────────────────

        On a phone the rail shows one tile at a time, so "the tile in the
        middle" is the tile being read — and nothing said which one that was.

        An IntersectionObserver whose ROOT is the rail and whose rootMargin
        squeezes it to a narrow band down the centre: the tile intersecting that
        band is the centred one. That is why this is an observer rather than a
        scroll handler doing arithmetic — the browser already knows what
        overlaps what, and it tells us off the main thread.

        Desktop shows four tiles at once, so there is no single "current" tile
        to mark and the observer is never created there.
      */
      const centreQuery = window.matchMedia('(max-width: 899px)');

      const observeCentre = () => {
        const key = '__hqRailCentreObserver';
        const previous = (rail as any)[key] as IntersectionObserver | undefined;
        if (previous) previous.disconnect();
        (rail as any)[key] = undefined;

        if (!centreQuery.matches) {
          rail.querySelectorAll('.is-centered').forEach((el) => el.classList.remove('is-centered'));
          return;
        }

        const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
          (entries) => {
            for (const entry of entries) {
              entry.target.classList.toggle('is-centered', entry.isIntersecting);
            }
          },
          { root: rail, rootMargin: '0px -45% 0px -45%', threshold: 0 },
        );

        rail.querySelectorAll('.feed-row-item').forEach((tile) => observer.observe(tile));
        (rail as any)[key] = observer;
      };

      observeCentre();
      centreQuery.addEventListener('change', observeCentre);

      /*
        Tapping the centred tile promotes it into the hero. The ring turns from
        white to red to confirm that, and only one tile in a rail can be the
        selected one.

        Marked here rather than in the hero's swap handler because it is a
        property of THIS rail — a click in one row must not clear the mark in
        another, and the hero does not know which rail it was handed an item
        from.
      */
      rail.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
        const tile = (event.target as Element)?.closest('.feed-row-item');
        if (!tile || !rail.contains(tile)) return;
        rail.querySelectorAll('.is-selected').forEach((el) => el.classList.remove('is-selected'));
        tile.classList.add('is-selected');
      });

      rail.addEventListener('scroll', sync, { passive: true });
      window.addEventListener('resize', () => {
        syncScrollPadding();
        sync();
      });

      syncScrollPadding();

      /*
        Undo the snap the browser already performed.

        On first layout the scroll-padding above has not been applied yet, so
        the snap engine treats the rail's inline padding as empty space and
        scrolls it away to align the first card to the bare padding box. Adding
        scroll-padding afterwards fixes future snaps but does not re-run that
        one, which left the rail resting at a non-zero scrollLeft — 32px, then
        8px once the padding landed mid-pass — with the first card sitting left
        of the heading it should line up with.

        Init only, deliberately. Doing this on resize would throw away a
        reader's place in the rail every time a phone rotated.
      */
      rail.scrollLeft = 0;

      sync();
    });
  }

  /*
    ─── ARRIVING WITH A ROW IN MIND ──────────────────────────────────────────

    /intel links to /feed#all-videos rather than to the video-only route, which
    had no way back out of itself. The hash names a row, and this takes the
    reader to it.

    Not left to the browser's native anchor jump: the rows are lazy in practice
    (images and the rotator settle after paint), so the element's position when
    the fragment resolves is not where it ends up. One frame plus a short beat
    is the difference between landing on the row and landing above it.

    `block: 'center'` rather than 'start' because the site header is fixed —
    'start' would put the row's heading underneath it.
  */
  /*
    ─── ARRIVING IS A JUMP; A HASH CHANGE IN PLACE IS A SCROLL ───────────────

    These are two different events and they used to share one answer: a smooth
    scroll, always. On ARRIVAL that is the second half of a lurch. The page
    transition has already animated the move, and then this smooth-scrolled
    several thousand pixels down a 7000px feed to reach the row, in a different
    axis from the slide that had just run. Reported as "way too jarring".

    So arrival lands instantly, underneath the transition: by the time the new
    page is painted it is already at the row, and the only motion the reader
    sees is the one the transition drew.

    A hashchange on the page you are ALREADY on has no transition to hide
    behind, and there the scroll IS the feedback that the click did something.
    That one stays smooth.
  */
  function scrollToHashRow(instant = false) {
    const id = window.location.hash.replace('#', '');
    if (!id) return;

    const row = document.getElementById(id) || document.querySelector(`[data-row="${CSS.escape(id)}"]`);
    if (!row) return;

    const reduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
    const behavior = instant || reduced ? 'auto' : 'smooth';
    
    if (instant) {
      row.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'auto', block: 'center' });
    } else {
      setTimeout(() => row.scrollIntoView({ behavior, block: 'center' }), 150);
    }
  }

  /* Same reason as /featured's: a hash change on the page you are already on is
     not a navigation, so `astro:page-load` never fires for it. */
  const ROW_HASH_KEY = '__hqFeedRowHashListener';
  const previousRowHashListener = (window as any)[ROW_HASH_KEY];
  if (previousRowHashListener) window.removeEventListener('hashchange', previousRowHashListener);
  /* Wrapped, not passed directly: the handler receives an Event, which would
     land in the `instant` parameter and be truthy. */
  const onRowHashChange = () => scrollToHashRow(false);
  (window as any)[ROW_HASH_KEY] = onRowHashChange;
  window.addEventListener('hashchange', onRowHashChange);

  document.addEventListener('astro:after-swap', () => {
    /* `true`: this is an arrival, so the row is already in place behind the
       page transition rather than scrolled to after it. Fires in after-swap
       so the browser captures the scrolled page for its View Transition
       snapshot, preventing a mid-animation jump. */
    scrollToHashRow(true);
  });

  /*
    ─── THE COLLAPSIBLE SECTIONS ARE GONE ────────────────────────────────────

    ~70 lines of accordion lived here. It existed because the page was six
    sections containing fourteen stacked ROWS, which made it an endless scroll.
    Eight horizontal rails do not stack that way: a rail of seventeen stories
    occupies one row of vertical space, which is the entire reason a browse
    surface uses them. There is nothing left to collapse.

    /featured keeps its accordion, and should: its rows are decks of hub cards,
    which DO stack.
  */

  document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', () => {
    initFeedRows();
    /* Fallback for hard reloads where after-swap doesn't fire */
    if (!document.documentElement.dataset.pageTransition) {
      scrollToHashRow(true);
    }
    
    /*
      ─── THE SPOTLIGHT SWAP MOVED TO THE HERO ────────────────────────────────

      Clicking a card to load it into the hero used to be wired up here, which
      tied it to this component: /category/<slug> mounts the same
      <FeedSpotlightHero /> but not this grid, so its cards were inert and its
      hero could only ever show the newest item.

      The behaviour belongs to the hero, not to whatever happens to be rendering
      cards beneath it, so it lives in FeedSpotlightHero.astro now and every
      page that mounts that hero gets it.
    */
  });
</script>
